{"id": "11658", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=11658", "title": "Fitzgerald", "text": "Fitzgerald is a small town in Georgia's Plantation Midlands region, and is the (get this) center of the \"Fitzgerald Micropolitan Area\". It was founded in 1895 by former Union Army drummer boy Philander H. Fitzgerald on behalf of Union Civil War veterans, leading to the nickname \"The Colony City\". You'll see the Civil War history in the street signs: Union generals on the east, Confederates on the west, and at the Blue &amp; Gray Museum. More importantly, though, tropical jungle chickens.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nFitzgerald is off I-75 at the intersection of US-129 and GA-107, an hour and a half drive south of Macon\u2014three hours from Atlanta. \nBy plane.\nIf you have your \"own\" wings, you can fly into Fitzgerald Municipal Airport[dead link] FAA LID: FZG.\nSee.\nWandering around town, keep an eye out for Red Junglefowl, a tropical pheasant from India or Burma or somewhere that looks an awful lot like a chicken, several of which were introduced for reasons well-masked by a Fitzgerald sense of humor. They have fled the boring old woods for the charming city center\u2014look for them downtown. Today they are the city mascot, loved and unloved by a whimsically ambivalent populace."}
{"id": "11659", "revid": "2248002", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=11659", "title": "Fitzgerald River National Park", "text": "Fitzgerald River National Park is in the Goldfields\u2013Esperance region of Western Australia. It is on the southern coast, and is accessed from the South Coast Highway.\nUnderstand.\nMost of the Wheatbelt region of Western Australia was cleared for agriculture between the 1890s and the 1950s, with \"awkward\" areas of land like Fitzgerald River NP, Stirling Range NP and many other smaller areas being too difficult to exploit. As a result, the national parks of the region were not planned, but happened because the exploitation was either not easy or not possible.\nFlora and fauna.\nThe most botanically important regions in the state with 1,800 known plant species in the park and 62 found only in the park.\nGet in.\nThe only way to get into Fitzgerald River National Park is by taking the South Coast Hwy from either Albany or Esperance and should take some hours to get here.\nFees and permits.\nA fee of $11 per car is collected at each entrance gates\nGet around.\nThe roads are graded gravel easily traversed without a 4WD. Some minor roads to remote areas are 4WD only. Occasionally roads may be closed if they have become flooded after wet weather.\nEat.\nThere are no places to eat in the park, and you will need to bring your own food.\nDrink.\nDrinking water is not available anywhere in the park\nSleep.\nCamping.\nThere are prescribed camp sites in the park. Only Point Ann and Four Mile Beach are accessible with a 2WD The campsites are only large enough to be suitable for tents or very small caravans.\nBackcountry.\nWhile it might be tempting to drop your tent on the beach, camping rough outside of the prescribed camp sites or in parking lots is not permitted.\nStay safe.\nFitzgerald River NP has had some very serious bushfires in the last few decades. Make sure you keep your possessions with you, and an easy escape route available if you are there in the bushfire season \u2013 take care if you see smoke, and always check any details about fires or other things to do with weather conditions and fire conditions before setting up camp."}
{"id": "11660", "revid": "2253620", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=11660", "title": "Fitzroy Island", "text": "Fitzroy Island[dead link] is one of the most visited islands of the Great Barrier Reef, close to Cairns.\nUnderstand.\nThe island is 3.39 km2 in size, 3.24 km2 of which is protected as Fitzroy Island National Park. The island is within sight of the mainland; in fact, it's a peak in a mountain chain which lies south of Cairns.\nGet in.\nFitzroy Island is only accessible by ferry from Cairns. Ferries depart at 8AM, 11AM and 1:30PM. The trip takes 45 minutes. A day return trip costs $82. Contact Fitzroy Island Resort. \u260f.. You can buy tickets that include some combination of snorkeling equipment hire, a barbecue lunch and a glass bottom boat tour.\nGet around.\nThe island's roads are for walking only; they're just dirt tracks of varying quality. Reception, the eateries and the accommodation are all very close to the ferry wharf, so you won't have to walk far with bags in tow. You can travel around the island by water in a sea kayak, or rent one of several kinds of boat.\nEat.\nThe resort has several small eating establishments:\nGo next.\nFerries to Cairns leave at 8AM, 11AM and 1:30PM"}
{"id": "11663", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=11663", "title": "Fiuggi", "text": "Fiuggi is a city in the south of Lazio in Italy.\nUnderstand.\nFiuggi is effectively two cities. One is a typical medieval Italian hill town, at 2500 feet above sea level. The other, at a slightly lower elevation, is a 20th-century spa town, called Fiuggi \"Terme\" (Baths). The water of Fiuggi Terme is bottled and distributed widely in Italy under the Fiuggi brand name. Fiuggi first became famous in the 14th century, when Pope Boniface VIII claimed his kidney stones had been healed by the waters but it was not until the 20th century that it became fashionable to \u201ctake the waters\u201d. The health benefits of many Government employees now include a trip to Fiuggi or a similar spa.\nGet in.\nFrom Rome, take the A1 Autostrada south in the direction of Naples. Exit at Anagni and follow signs to Fiuggi to the east.\nSleep.\nThere is an abundance of relatively cheap hotels in Fiuggi to cater to visitors to the Spa. Note that many are open only in the summer months. In the winter they close up and the staff head north to the Ski resorts."}
{"id": "11664", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=11664", "title": "Fiumefreddo di Sicilia", "text": "Fiumefreddo di Sicilia is a city in the province of Catania on the north eastern slopes of Etna on the Ionian coast. \nUnderstand.\nThe territory is almost entirely flat, especially in the part that extends towards the sea and the beach of Marina di Cottone.\nIt has a Mediterranean climate. Winter ensures generally mild temperatures during the day, but the difference in temperatures compared to the night-time hours can also be quite pronounced. The long-lasting summer is very hot, sometimes with high levels of humidity. Precipitation reaches a very marked summer minimum and a moderate peak in the autumn season.\nGet in.\nFiumefreddo di Sicilia is served by a junction of the Messina - Catania highway and is crossed by the north-south direction (A18, SS114).\nThrough it (SS120), you can reach Etna and the Madonie.\nGet around.\nDuring the summer, shuttle services are set up towards the seaside resort of Marina di Cottone."}
{"id": "11665", "revid": "210638", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=11665", "title": "Fiumicino", "text": "Fiumicino is a city in Lazio. This city is on the coast near to Rome, and close to the airport that bears its name.\nUnderstand.\nFiumicino was originally a fishing village and is still an important fishing center. It is situated on the west coast of Italy, in the province of Rome. The town is home to approximately 60,000 inhabitants.\nThis region enjoys a Mediterranean climate similar to that of Rome, with occasional sea breezes to keep it cooler in summer. Spring and autumn are the best time to visit as the weather is most pleasant at this time. In the peak of summers, the temperature tends to regularly go over 35\u00b0C. Winters are mild.\nLately, it has also become a popular retreat for people from Rome. It is also a popular place for visitors to stay while visiting Rome. If you are flying out from Fiumicino airport it could be a pleasant place to stay your last night, avoiding the hassle of Rome's traffic on the way to catch your flight.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nRome's Leonardo da Vinci International Airport () is close by and is more normally referred to as Fiumicino Airport. The center of Fiumicino is located only a short drive away from the airport. However, taxis will probably refuse to take you there. They will probably have been queueing for two hours expecting at least a \u20ac40 fare to the center of Rome and will not want a \u20ac10-\u20ac12 fare to Fiumicino. If the first taxi refuses to take you, try another.\nBy boat.\nBattelli di Roma: A great way to go from Rome to Ostia and at a very reasonable rate. The boat picks up/drops off in Ostia Antica, Fiumicino's neighboring town.\nBy train.\nThere is no train station in the city center. The nearest train station is at the airport. Alternatively, you can get off at Ostia Antica station on the Roma-Lido suburban railway line. From there it is about a 15min car ride to Fiumicino."}
{"id": "11666", "revid": "160739", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=11666", "title": "Five Blues Lake National Park", "text": "Five Blues Lake National Park is on the border between the Stann Creek and Cayo districts of Belize. Read that name carefully: the park has a lake with five shades of blue, not five lakes all the same color (\"Five Blue Lakes\"). It is known for its wildlife, caves, and of course the lake.\nUnderstand.\nFive Blues Lake National Park is unusual in that it's managed entirely by the local people of Saint Margaret's Village, with a volunteer board of directors drawn from the various ethnic groups of this small community.\nHistory.\nThe park was established on Earth Day in 1991.\nLandscape.\nThe geographic centerpiece of this 4200 acre park is Five Blues Lake, a cenote formed by the collapse of a huge underground cave. The varying depth (up to 200 feet) and composition of the bottom, and the light filtering through surrounding trees give the water different colors, which is the origin of the lake's name: Five Blues.\nGet in.\nAlthough located in Stann Creek, it is accessed via Saint Margaret's Village in Cayo district."}
{"id": "11673", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=11673", "title": "Fj\u00e6rland", "text": "Fj\u00e6rland is a tiny town at the end of the Fj\u00e6rlandsfjord, an arm of the Sognefjord. In Sogn og Fjordane county. It is a particularly enjoyable gateway to the Jostedalsbreen National Park, and is close to two glacial tongues.\nUnderstand.\nIf you want a close-up view of a glacier without actually climbing on it, then Fj\u00e6rland is the place to go. In addition to this, Fj\u00e6rland is Norway's \"bokbyen\" - book town. Almost every shop in the town sells a range of new and used books, and there is even an \"honesty book shop\", not far from the ferry stop, where books are sold from a stand without any staff.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe closest option is Sogndal airport ('). Alternatively F\u00f8rde airport (').\nBy car.\nFj\u00e6rland is 38 km northwest of Sogndal on route 5 on the way to Skei, which is on E39.\nBy boat.\nIn summer, Fj\u00e6rland can be reached by ferry from Balestrand, a trip that gives ample photo opportunities - there's an amazing view of the Josteldalsbreen glacier behind the fjord as you approach the town.\nBy bus.\nThere are buses each day to nearby towns, including Sogndal and Skei, both five times daily. The bus stop is 2 km out of the town centre, just across the road from the Norwegian glacier museum. It's a bit of a walk, so leave ample time to avoid missing what might be your only bus out of there.\nGet around.\nA bus leaves from the ferry port once a day in May and September, twice a day in June\u2013August, taking passengers firstly to the Glacier Museum and then to the glacier. Alternatively, catch a taxi, hire a bike at the tourist office, or use your own car."}
{"id": "11675", "revid": "265484", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=11675", "title": "Fjerritslev", "text": "Fjerritslev is a small town in North Jutland, approximately halfway between Thisted and Aalborg. It is the main town of the Han Herred region, although only 3,300 people live here (2026), which explains how thinly populated this region is.\nUnderstand.\nHan Herred (or Hanherred) is the strip of land between the North Sea and the Limfjord, connecting the Thy region in the west to the Vendsyssel region in the east. Han Herred translates as \"Han Shire\", or \"Han Hundred\", in English.\nBecause of the sparse population in and around Fjerritslev, not much is going on here, but the region has a deep history, and the landscapes are beautiful in late spring and summer. If you include the whole region of Han Herred, there are several points of interest to travellers around here.\nThe nature and coastal landscapes are the most notable attractions in the Han Herred region. There are several options for camping, both organised camping grounds and in wild nature, but to stock up on groceries and basic necessities, you will need to go to the towns of Fjerritslev, Brovst, Tranum, or Fr\u00f8strup. This is also where you will find charging stations for your car or fossil fuels.\nAs the rest of the North Sea coast in Denmark, The North Sea coast in Han Herred is popular with holiday-makers in the summer, but not as crowded as most other places. \nFjerritslev probably started out as a farming village in Medieval times. When the road between Aalborg and Thisted was built in the 1830s, the town began to grow and develop. People have lived in Han Herred since the Stone Ages, as testified by ancient barrows and archaeological finds.\nGet in.\nCar or bus is the easiest way to reach Fjerritslev.\nFjerritslev is reached by the \"Prim\u00e6rrute 11\" (Primary Route 11) road from either Aalborg or Thisted, or it is reached by the \"Prim\u00e6rrute 29\" (Primary Route 29) from the town of Aars in Himmerland, via the \"Aggersundbroen\" bridge straddling the Limfjord north of L\u00f8gst\u00f8r. You can also reach the region from the island of Mors directly by the small Feggesund car-ferry at the Amtoft Harbour southwest of Fjerritslev.\nBy bus.\nNordjyllands Traffikselskab serves Fjerritslev and Han Herred with public busses, both locally and regional, but the service is very limited on school holidays, check their website for details.\nOutside school holidays, you can get in by bus from Hobro by Aars and the Aggersundbroen bridge in the south, from Thisted in the west, and from Aalborg in the east. There is also a route from the north by the small villages of Saltum and Kaas close to Blokhus.\nBy bicycle.\nTwo national bicycle routes pass through Han Herred.\nGet around.\nFjerritslev can easily be done on two legs. Vestergade and \u00d8stergade are the main shopping streets of the small town.\nIf you want to visit other destinations in Han Herred, a car or a bicycle is a better option, depending on how far you need to go. You can find charging and gas stations in the towns of Fjerritslev, Brovst, Tranum, and Fr\u00f8strup.\nLocal public buses don't run on school holidays, which coincide with peak tourism seasons.\nSee.\nTowns and settlements.\nThere are only four towns in the region: Fjerritslev, Brovst, Tranum, and Fr\u00f8strup, but there are points of interest scattered throughout the countryside as well.\nBrovst.\nThe second largest town in Han Herred with a little more than 2,600 residents. It is a small market town east of Fjerritslev and boasts a beautiful, historic manor: The Bratskov Manor House. Outside the town itself, you will find the Medieval Kokkedal Castle, and the former monastery Oxholm. \nJernbanegade is the central high street.\nFr\u00f8strup.\nSmall village with just about 600 residents. This is where local farmers go to get their machines fixed. Fr\u00f8strup is the nearest shopping opportunity for visitors to the Bulbjerg nature site.\nSettlements.\n - Small, quiet fishing settlement in a remote spot near Bulbjerg. Go here, if you want to enjoy a quiet time in nature, or visit on a long-distance hike as a stop over. There are a few bead and breakfast options and several summer houses in the area might be available for rent. Lild Strand is a good choice, if you want to stay overnight in the Bulbjerg area and don't want to camp out in a tent. In the darker part of the year, starting from September, several group activities related to the Bulbjerg Dark Sky Park are arranged from Lild Strand. \n - Hamlet southeast of Fjerritslev. A nice stop if you are going to explore the Tingskoven forest.\nHistoric monuments.\nThere are several historic monuments in the region. They are hardly worth a long detour, but might be an interesting visit if you happen to be in their vicinity.\nDo.\nBeaches.\nGo to the beach on the North Sea coast, there are several good spots in this region. Great option for a beautiful sunset experience year round. The entire Jammerbugt Bay is an important commercial fishing spot in Denmark, and has been for centuries. While the vast majority of the catch is landed in Hanstholm and Hirtshals, you can also find a couple of smaller landing spots around Han Herred, and the locals are proud of their history and keeps the traditional ways of life alive here.\nThe North Sea beaches in Han Herred are not crowded in the summer, even though the water quality is excellent and as good as it gets (check here). In most places, the beach is broad, waters are shallow and quite warm in the summer (20 C and up in late June and July); perfect for beach and water activities. The downside is that gravel and small stones can dominate some stretches, in contrast to the beaches near Blokhus further north for example. \nHiking.\nFjerritslev and the Han Herred region offers many great opportunities for nature hikes. The landscapes are varied and most hikes are relatively short and easy. Late May to October is the most beautiful time of year. \nWatch out for snakes (European Adder) in the heathlands. They are neither abundant nor aggressive, and if you stick to the trails, are observant, or wear boots and long trousers, you will be completely safe. In case you get bitten, it is good to know that only 10-15% of bites lead to serious complications, but call the emergency line in any case. Don't touch the bite area, don't move around, and remain as calm as possible. Children, weak, or allergic people are the most vulnerable.\nThe long-range hiking trails of H\u00e6rvejen, Nords\u00f8stien, and Vestkyststien pass through the coastal landscapes north of Fjerritslev and you can connect to them at several spots:\nBuy.\nBecause of the rural conditions, it's important to know where you can buy basic groceries and where you can refuel your car or motor vehicle.\nIn Fjerritslev, these shops might be good to know:\nEat.\nFjerritslev doesn't have many eateries, so as a tourist, unless you have booked an all-inclusive stay at a hotel, or are going to cook your own food at a camp site every day, it is good to know where you can have breakfast, lunch, and evening meals around Han Herred. \nDanish food.\nYou can have Danish specialities even at the fine restaurants around Han Herred, meals like \"sm\u00f8rrebr\u00f8d\", \"stjerneskud\", or \"stegt r\u00f8dsp\u00e6tte med persillesovs\", but only a few places have a specific focus on traditional Danish cuisine.\nSeafood.\nSeafood is a large part of the menus around Han Herred, at simple food stalls and fancy restaurants. Go to Thorup Strand to buy your own fresh seafood at the fishmongers, straight from the North Sea.\nA classic, traditional Danish summertime seafood dish tied to the North Sea coast is \"stegt r\u00f8dsp\u00e6tte med persillesovs\", which comprise breaded and fried plaice, served with white parsley sauce and new potatoes. It is simple, elegant, and very tasty if done right. Go to one of the high-end restaurants to try it.\nSleep.\nThere aren't many options to stay in the town of Fjerritslev itself, but the region has plenty, especially camp sites.\nSlettestrand.\nSlettestrand just north of Fjerritslev is a popular beach resort in the summer and has plenty of accommodation.\nGo next.\nFjerritslev is on the Primary Route 11 road between Aalborg and Thisted, about 50 km each way."}
{"id": "11680", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=11680", "title": "Flagler Beach", "text": "Flagler Beach is a town in Florida's First Coast.\nUnderstand.\nNestled on Florida's east coast between Daytona Beach and St. Augustine, the flourishing City of Flagler Beach is home to over 5,411. residents. Since its incorporation in April, 1925, Flagler Beach has grown from a sleepy fishing village to a unique seaside community while still retaining much of its original charm.\nThe City is home to six miles of uncrowded beaches, many great fishing spots including the Flagler Beach Municipal Pier, restaurants with ocean front dining, gift shops and a whole host of recreational facilities. The Intracoastal Waterway also flows through the town providing countless opportunities for boaters and fishermen.\nGet in.\nA1A, the beach road, goes through the city from north to south and is the main route to the beach town. It goes north toward St. Augustine and south toward Ormond Beach."}
{"id": "11682", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=11682", "title": "Flagstaff", "text": "\"For other places with the same name, see Flagstaff (disambiguation).\"\nFlagstaff is a city of over 75,000 people (2019) near the San Francisco Peaks mountain range of northern Arizona and the Grand Canyon. At an altitude of 6990 ft, Flagstaff and much of the surrounding region are substantially cooler than the low desert that dominates the southern part of the state. Though still dry by east coast standards, enough rain and snow falls in the area to allow a forest of ponderosa pine trees to cover the landscape. Winters tend to be cold, and heavy snowfall is expected on an annual basis.\nUnderstand.\nClimate.\nAlthough located in Arizona, Flagstaff has a very different climate from the southern desert areas of the state. Winters are cold and sometimes very snowy, depending on the year. Temperatures can dip below 0 F in the coldest months. Snow tends to melt quickly, as the temperature is usually above freezing during the day, even in winter. The spring season is windy and chilly, albeit otherwise mild, with occasional rain and snowstorms.\nFlagstaff's summer begins around May, when temperatures warm substantially; the highs can exceed 90 F during the hottest days. During the monsoon season (roughly June - September), intense, frequent rainstorms occur, lowering temperatures and ending the dry spell that tends to set in when the weather warms. The cool temperatures and frequent rain continue into the fall, and the first snowfall is often in November or December.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nFlagstaff is at the intersection of I-40, which runs west to California and east to New Mexico (following historic Route 66) and I-17, which runs south to Phoenix.\nBy plane.\n () American Airlines provides service from Dallas-Fort Worth and Phoenix Sky Harbor as the only commercial airline to Flagstaff. Otherwise the airport serves a general aviation airport.\nThe nearest major airport is Phoenix Sky Harbor International Airport (). Driving time between Flagstaff and Sky Harbor is greater than the 150-mile separation would suggest: the airport is on the east side of Phoenix, traffic jams in Phoenix are a problem, and I-17 can be shutdown for accidents or wildfires. Allow 2\u00bd hours or more to get from one to the other by car.\nGet around.\nFlagstaff is stretched out along historic Route 66, which runs roughly east-west. The fairly small (about 5 blocks square) downtown is on the western side of town, near the base of Mars Hill, the location of Lowell Observatory. The I-40 highway and the BNSF railroad tracks run roughly parallel to Route 66. Northern Arizona University is south of downtown and the tracks, but north of I-40. Here the north-south oriented Milton Road, which becomes I-17, is the main thoroughfare, with Route 66 heading west north of the railroad underpass.\nThe city is fairly bike-friendly, with many roads having bike lanes. In 2006 Flagstaff was designated a \"Bicycle-Friendly Community\" by the League of American Bicyclists. The Flagstaff Urban Trails System (FUTS) includes more than 50 miles of paved and unpaved trails that wind throughout the town and are used extensively for recreation and transportation. One well-traveled FUTS path runs along the south side of Route 66 from downtown to the east side of town.\nThe Northern Arizona Intergovernmental Public Transportation Authority operates The Mountain Line, fixed-route bus service that extends throughout Flagstaff and carries more than one million passengers per year.\nDo.\nOutdoors.\nThe area surrounding Flagstaff is a hotspot for outdoor activities.\nDrink.\nFor beer fans, Flagstaff boasts several brewpubs and microbreweries.\nSleep.\nBecause of its proximity to the Grand Canyon, Flagstaff has a wide array of hotels and other lodging, with over 5,000 rooms available. Cheap rooms are available at older non-chain motels, but you get what you pay for, and prices may not be that much cheaper than discount chains. Be sure to shop around and bargain as proprietors are often willing to drop prices during the off-season.\nMany motels of the older variety are located along Route 66 east of downtown. More older hotels and most newer motels are located south of downtown along Milton Road. There are a few historic hotels downtown, as well as two hostels. Various B&amp;B establishments can be found near downtown in older neighborhoods. Campgrounds and RV parks can be found on the outskirts of town. Note that the BNSF rail line is very busy and in many hotels train horn noise is prominent (though not overwhelming as no facility is directly on the tracks). Sensitive sleepers should look to the Milton Road area for more quiet. Visible from the I-40 freeway off the Butler Ave. exit on Lucky Lane is a large selection of national chain economy class hotels including Econo Lodge, Motel 6, Quality Inn, Super 8 and Howard Johnson.\nCamping.\nDispersed camping (free) is permitted almost anywhere in the Coconino National Forest, which abuts town in many places (i.e. Thorpe Park, Mt Elden). Specific sites set aside for dispersed camping include Cinder Hills, Freidlein Prairie, and Marshall Lake (free, no water or facilities). Some of the choicest camp sites are on the San Francisco peaks and inner basin where a breathtaking aspen color changes happen every fall.\nStay safe.\nAlthough Flagstaff is well within the southern half of the U.S., it receives more snowfall than just about any other American city. Always come prepared in wintertime.\nThere is not a huge crime problem, but certain areas noted below should be avoided, mainly at night:\nSunnyside, one of Flagstaff's larger neighborhoods, is on the east side of town (bordered by Cedar Ave. on the north, 4th St. on the east, Izabel St. and Cedar Hill on the west and Route 66 on the south). Sunnyside is an incredibly diverse and interesting neighborhood but is also one of the most poverty-stricken neighborhoods in the city. Most points of interest are restaurants located on the edge of the neighborhood in the business districts which are generally safe any time of day. The area has been afflicted with some gang activity, mostly in the very center of the neighborhood. Just don't walk the neighborhood at night or hang out in an area that seems unsafe.\nThe other area that becomes unsafe at night is some areas of what locals call the \"South Side\". South of the railroad tracks, west of Lone Tree Road, east of Milton and north of the NAU campus is the general designation of this area. The exception being most of the areas along South Beaver St. and South San Francisco St., which are safe even at night because of the night-time music, restaurant and bar scene that takes place along these streets. Areas closer to campus and on side streets such as South O'Leary and South Fontaine should be avoided at night. Though a lot of college students inhabit the area, drug addicts and transients do also, and there is some gang activity.\nGo next.\nAn unusual number of national parks and monuments are close to Flagstaff, the largest and most famous of which is the Grand Canyon, but there are three right nearby. Note that there is a $25 Flagstaff Area National Monuments Annual Pass which works for all three monuments, and covers entrance fees for up to four people which may be cheaper for groups visiting multiple sites.\nFor more of the canyon landscape: the Grand Canyon is just a short drive further north, and to the northeast is Navajo Country and Vermilion Cliffs National Monument.\nThe detour through Sedona on the way south to Phoenix offers a scenic route west of I-17.\nFor more forested high-country, head southeast to the Mogollon Rim area or southwest towards Prescott.\nFor more of Historic Route 66 and the Old West, head east towards New Mexico, going through Williams and onwards to Gallup, Albuquerque and Santa Fe. Or head west to Kingman, with options to continue into California towards Barstow and eventually Los Angeles, or veer north at Kingman from Route 66 to Hoover Dam and Las Vegas."}
